WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.777
Here is a thread pool with two core threads, a maximum of four, and a queue that holds two tasks.

2
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.129
Seven tasks arrive, one by one.

3
00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:13.184
Each one takes half a second.

4
00:00:13.184 --> 00:00:14.884
Where does each task go?

5
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.833
Every call to execute walks the same four rules, in this order.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.012
Task 1 arrives.

7
00:00:21.012 --> 00:00:26.072
The pool has fewer threads than core, so rule 1 starts a new thread for it.

8
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:28.483
Task 2 also gets a new thread.

9
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:32.104
Rule 1 starts one even if another thread is idle.

10
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:32.849
Task 3.

11
00:00:32.849 --> 00:00:34.984
The pool is at core size now.

12
00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:39.329
So rule 2 offers the task to the queue, and it waits there.

13
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.860
Task 4 waits too.

14
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.576
Now the queue is full.

15
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.331
Task 5.

16
00:00:43.331 --> 00:00:44.697
The queue says no.

17
00:00:44.697 --> 00:00:50.692
Rule 3 starts an extra thread, because three threads is within the max of four.

18
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:53.062
Task 6 gets the fourth thread.

19
00:00:53.062 --> 00:00:54.979
The pool is now at its max.

20
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.319
Task 7 finds everything full.

21
00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:00.316
Rule 4 hands it to the rejection handler.

22
00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:04.116
The default one throws a RejectedExecutionException.

23
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:05.567
Read rule 3 again.

24
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.200
The pool grows past core only when the queue is full.

25
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.393
Most people expect the opposite.

26
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:14.383
And tasks 5 and 6 run before tasks 3 and 4.

27
00:01:14.383 --> 00:01:19.355
A new thread takes the task that made it, not the oldest task in the queue.

28
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.253
Now swap in an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue.

29
00:01:23.253 --> 00:01:25.979
That is the queue newFixedThreadPool uses.

30
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.711
Rule 2 always succeeds.

31
00:01:27.711 --> 00:01:30.797
Every task past core just waits in the queue.

32
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:35.115
So rule 3 never runs, and no extra thread is ever started.

33
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:38.648
Rule 4 never runs either, so nothing is rejected.

34
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.315
Set max to 100 and you still get two threads, forever.

35
00:01:42.315 --> 00:01:44.659
Under overload the queue just grows.

36
00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:47.588
The first alert you get is probably the heap.

37
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:55.463
A bounded queue with a finite max is the shape that pushes back: rule 4 can fire, and overload becomes visible.
